      \field{abstract}{Scene Graph Generation ({SGG}) provides basic language representation of visual scenes, requiring models to grasp complex and diverse semantics between objects. This complexity and diversity in {SGG} leads to underrepresentation, where parts of triplet labels are rare or even unseen during training, resulting in imprecise predictions. To tackle this, we propose integrating the pretrained Vision-language Models to enhance representation. However, due to the gap between pretraining and {SGG}, direct inference of pretrained {VLMs} on {SGG} leads to severe bias, which stems from the imbalanced predicates distribution in the pretraining language set. To alleviate the bias, we introduce a novel {LM} Estimation to approximate the unattainable predicates distribution. Finally, we ensemble the debiased {VLMs} with {SGG} models to enhance the representation, where we design a certainty-aware indicator to score each sample and dynamically adjust the ensemble weights. Our training-free method effectively addresses the predicates bias in pretrained {VLMs}, enhances {SGG}'s representation, and significantly improve the performance.}
